A||N||9||chester||Backfire after WOT||||5||
Z||000000||chester||10-04-2001||09:33 AM||||I've had this happen on occasion.  Very annoying.  If I get into it (WOT) no matter what gear and let off abruptly (normally to hit the brakes) I get a backfire. Any ideas on what would cause this?  I have a built 2.8 running 19# injectors.  One thing that I am lacking is back pressure on the exhaust.  Could this be part of my problem?  <BR>Any ideas?<P>Thanks<P>Rob D.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||5
Z||000001||falconhulk||10-04-2001||10:22 AM||||I had this problem with my firebird.  it was caused by parts of the exasust system haing rusted holes in it.  I assume you already checked this though?  If so exaust backfire is caused by unused fuel igniting in the exaust system.  This could mean you are running to rich at WOT.  After revving the engine and and letting off there should be unused fuel expelled but not enough to ignite without an external source (tail pipe igniter).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||10-04-2001||10:36 AM||||Do you have a custom PROM with those 19# injectors? If not then I guess the stock PROM is opening the 19# injectors just like the stock ones and you will have plenty of unused fuel with just a 2.8. I have a 3.4 with 2.8 injectors and it doesn't lean out. Some TPIs V8 (or 305) use 19# injectors and don't get lean. Even 2.8s with basic nitro/turbo setups. My point is that no one have convinced me of the need of bigger injectors on a 2.8 unless you force-feed it big time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||chester||10-04-2001||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>Do you have a custom PROM with those 19# injectors? If not then I guess the stock PROM is opening the 19# injectors just like the stock ones and you will have plenty of unused fuel with just a 2.8. I have a 3.4 with 2.8 injectors and it doesn't lean out. Some TPIs V8 (or 305) use 19# injectors and don't get lean. Even 2.8s with basic nitro/turbo setups. My point is that no one have convinced me of the need of bigger injectors on a 2.8 unless you force-feed it big time.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I put 19# injectors in it along with an adjustable pressure regulator in prep for a turbo.  I'm going with an Accel DFI setup so if this is due to excessive fuel (which I did have till I bumped the pressure down) I'll be able to dial it in with the ECM. I currently have a stage III Hypertech chip in it.  I don't think that it is doing a good job with all the mods I did ie. cam, heads etc. since it is designed to run a stock motor. My intentions are getting the DFI wired in and having it Dyno tuned.  That should eliminate any engine management issues. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||chester||10-04-2001||11:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>I had this problem with my firebird.  it was caused by parts of the exasust system haing rusted holes in it.  I assume you already checked this though?  If so exaust backfire is caused by unused fuel igniting in the exaust system.  This could mean you are running to rich at WOT.  After revving the engine and and letting off there should be unused fuel expelled but not enough to ignite without an external source (tail pipe igniter).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well the exhaust is not finished yet.  I have a set of custom headers that have a 2 1/4" out on the cross over pipe.  Currently it is attached to the stock exhaust which is 2" .  Needless to say it is an ill fit and does leak.  That is getting addressed with a complete 2 1/4" exhaust from the cross over pipe back to a flowmaster hopefully tomorrow. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||10-04-2001||11:24 AM||||Chester, I have headers (FOCOA) and the stage III hypertech too. You are doing the right thing with the dyno tuning an DFI. That should make it easier to correct. In the mean time wear a cowboy hat while shooting  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Please let us know of your results.||||reg||1
Z||000006||kinboyatuwo||10-04-2001||12:26 PM||||just an idea but your cat could be dead, and i dont mean unde your wheel<BR>if it is not burning all the ejected feul than that extra bypassing could be just enough at WOT to ignite<P>just an idea<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/b4lower50.jpg"> <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||4
Z||000007||chester||10-04-2001||12:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kinboyatuwo:<BR><B>just an idea but your cat could be dead, and i dont mean unde your wheel<BR>if it is not burning all the ejected feul than that extra bypassing could be just enough at WOT to ignite<P>just an idea<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, now that you mention it, the cat has been dead and buried for some time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  The cat was "eliminated" some time ago.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||falconhulk||10-04-2001||01:04 PM||||A hole the exhaust system can cause the oxygen sensor in the fuel injection system to falsely sense a lean fuel mixture. The computer then injects extra fuel into the engine to compensate. Unburned fuel passes through the engine into the exhaust system and combines with the oxygen let in by the exhaust leak to cause a loud backfire.  Sounds like your problem to me!<P>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-04-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||10-05-2001||07:31 AM||||Any performance engine should do this when running properly.  My 2.8 does (heh, surprisingly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Ferrari's 3.5litre and decendant V8s all dop it (348, 355, 360) so to TRs and 308/328s too for that matter...<P>Lotus Esprit V8s sure do, they even SPIT FIRE!  As do Ferrari 333SPs, and most of the 900P series lemans cars, all the time.<P>It's just unburnt fuel igniting in your eshaust/sneaking past your edhaust valves still burning. Nothing to worry about really.  It's an artifact of getting rid of the injector-cutoff that most stock production engine management systems use.  Good riddance, that thing makes valves dry out, and makes surgy cars. blah.<P><BR>(as a test, try a stock prom maybe. if it's even close. *should* have a fuel cutoff, though 88s don't I belive...)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
